# builder.fayth — the Guardian: the only persona that writes code.
#
# A fayth is a persona DEFINITION; an aeon is one summoned instance of it. Everything a
# persona is amounts to four things: a predicate that carves its partition out of the
# graph, the statutes it must read, the tools it may use, and what wakes it.

FAYTH_NAME=builder
FAYTH_DESC="Guardian — claims ready Spira plan beads and implements them"

# The partition. AND-labels, so this can only ever see beads deliberately marked as Spira
# plan work — never beads imported from a predecessor alongside them, which are a replica
# that the predecessor is still actively working.
FAYTH_LABELS="spira,plan"
FAYTH_EXCLUDE_LABELS="spira-poison,$SPIRA_ASK_LABEL,$SPIRA_CI_LABEL"

# Statutes only. SOPs are runbooks for production incidents; a builder will never execute
# one, and a shelf of them would push the law it does have to obey off the end of the
# budget. Ops reads "law-,sop-".
FAYTH_MEMORY_PREFIXES="law-"

# NO FAYTH_REPO. The repository comes from the BEAD — a `repo:<name>` label resolved
# through .claude/spira/repo-map — because a repository pinned per persona is a
# persona that can only ever work one of them, and every fayth here named the same
# one. A bead with no `repo:` label is the home repo, which is what Spira's own plan
# beads are.
FAYTH_MODEL=claude-opus-5
# Four (per the operator, 2026-09-07). The progression has been two, then three, then this,
# each time against a queue that kept growing: 15 ready at two, 47 at three, 52 at four with
# every slot busy and the sentinel logging "at concurrency cap" every pass.
#
# THE GOVERNOR DISAGREES AND IS READ CORRECTLY. At the time of this change it reported
# SP_HEADROOM=0 — "no headroom: cpu 26% idle (avg), floor 25%, 25% per aeon" — on a four-core
# box at load 4.94. It is in `measure` mode, so it reports and does not clamp. The operator's
# reading is that the fourth aeon does not ADD load, it inherits it: three aeons plus his own
# interactive session is what that 26% is measuring, and the fourth starts when he stops.
# The five-hour account window, not the box, remains the real ceiling.
#
# Aeons work in separate worktrees and claim atomically, so the risk is contention in a
# shared repo, not lost work; the governor will withhold in enforce mode if that proves wrong.
# SPIRA_MAX_AEONS in spira.conf is NOT this number: it is declared and documented but nothing
# reads it, so this file is the only ceiling that exists.
FAYTH_MAX_CONCURRENT=4

# The lease TTL the claim carries, and how often the aeon refreshes it. Heartbeats write
# no Dolt commit, so a fast cadence is free; the reaper's grace window is ~2x the TTL.
FAYTH_LEASE_MINUTES=90
FAYTH_HEARTBEAT_SECONDS=120

# NO WALL-CLOCK CEILING. An aeon runs until its work is done. A clock cannot distinguish
# slow from stuck — a bead whose CI takes four rounds is not failing — and killing on one
# burned an attempt toward the poison threshold for being legitimately long. Stuck is
# detected by the heartbeat, which fires only while something observably moves and stops
# when nothing has for FAYTH_STALL_BEATS checks; the lease then expires and bd reclaim
# returns the bead. Set FAYTH_TIMEOUT_SECONDS only for a persona that genuinely needs a
# hard bound.
FAYTH_STALL_BEATS=10

FAYTH_TOOLS="Bash,Read,Edit,Write,Glob,Grep,TodoWrite"
